.TH XFER 1 "" "xfer 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
xfer \- interactive remote file transfer client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xfer
.br
.B xfer attach
.RI [ id ]
.br
.B xfer
.BR \-f " | " \-\-file
.I file
.br
.B xfer
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.br
.B xfer
.BR \-V " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xfer
is an interactive, terminal-based file transfer program for S3 and
S3-compatible storage, SFTP, SMB, and WebDAV. It provides a command prompt,
remote and local working directories, listings, recursive transfers,
completion, a file selector, a pager, and job control.
.PP
Without arguments, xfer starts a new per-session daemon and attaches the
terminal to it. Establish a connection with the
.B open
command. The daemon owns the connection, settings, and jobs; detaching the
terminal does not cancel running transfers. A detached daemon exits when
it has no running jobs.
.PP
The interface is modeled after
.BR lftp (1),
but xfer is not a drop-in replacement. In particular, it does not implement
FTP, FTPS, most of lftp's batch options, or lftp's shell command language.
.SS Normal startup
For normal use, simply run:
.PP
.nf
.RS
xfer
.RE
.fi
.PP
This automatically creates a daemon and a terminal client connected to it.
You do not need to start the daemon separately or attach manually.
Detaching and reattaching are optional features for leaving jobs running
and returning to them later.
.SS Command scripts
.BI "xfer -f " file
starts a fresh daemon, runs commands from the named file, and exits.
Each nonblank, non-comment line is one command, using the same quoting as
the interactive prompt. Commands execute in order and each job finishes
before the next command starts. Script output goes to standard output;
the pager and command history are not used. The usual
.I ~/.xferrc
is not read in script mode, so scripts do not depend on interactive startup
commands. The daemon still reads its normal configuration files.
.PP
A failed or cancelled job, malformed or unknown command, failed Bash
escape, or unavailable interactive operation stops the script with a
nonzero exit status and a file and line diagnostic. Some commands report
usage or setting errors as ordinary output rather than a failure status;
such messages do not stop the script. Do not rely on the exit status alone
to detect those errors.
.PP
Interactive password prompts, bookmark editing, and interactive job-control
commands are not available. Use noninteractive credentials instead.
.B exit
or
.B detach
ends the script early. Lines starting with
.B !
run Bash and a nonzero Bash exit status stops the script.
.SS Attaching to a session
.B xfer attach
attaches to the only ready detached daemon. If several are available, it
lists their instance IDs and asks you to select one. With
.IR id ,
it selects an exact instance ID or an unambiguous ID prefix.
Only one terminal may be attached to a daemon at a time.
.SS Command syntax
Enter one command per line. Arguments use shell-like quoting and backslash
escaping; for example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cd 'photo album'
get 'annual report.pdf'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Ordinary xfer commands do not perform shell variable or command expansion,
pipelines, redirection, command grouping, or chaining with semicolons,
.BR && ,
or
.BR || .
A trailing
.B &
is not a background operator. Use Ctrl-Z to leave a job running in the
background. Commands beginning with
.B !
are an exception: their remaining text is passed unchanged to Bash.
.PP
Where supported,
.B \-\-
ends option parsing so that subsequent names beginning with a hyphen are
operands. Quoting a wildcard does not disable a command's own pattern
matching. The
.B get
and
.B put
commands match patterns against files in the current remote or local
directory, respectively, using * for any sequence and ? for one character;
other characters are literal. Prefix a name with
.B ./
to bypass their pattern expansion.
.SS Jobs and retries
Remote operations run as daemon jobs. The client normally waits for a
started job in the foreground. Ctrl-C requests cancellation of the
foreground job; Ctrl-Z returns to the prompt without stopping it.
Use
.B jobs
and
.B status
for information,
.B fg
to wait for a job again, and
.B cancel
to cancel a background job.
.PP
Retryable operations use backoff for transient failures. This is not a
transaction guarantee: mutating operations such as
.B mv
and
.B rm
are not automatically replayed. A failed or cancelled operation can leave
partial files or an ambiguous remote result. Inspect the destination
before retrying.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI ! command
Run a local Bash command with the terminal's standard input, output, and
error streams. The working directory is the daemon's current local
directory. Bash handles quoting, expansion, pipelines, and redirection.
This is a client-side command, not a daemon job.
.TP
.B bg
Leave jobs in the background. All daemon jobs are already background tasks;
this command does not resume a stopped operating-system process.
.TP
.B bookmark \fR[\fBedit\fR | \fBreload\fR]
Without arguments, list bookmark names and addresses, omitting credentials.
.B edit
opens the TOML bookmark file using
.BR VISUAL ,
then
.BR EDITOR ,
or
.BR vi .
A missing file is created with private permissions.
.B reload
validates the file. Bookmarks are read afresh on each connection, so edits
do not require a daemon restart and do not change an existing connection.
.TP
.BI cancel " job-id"
Request cancellation of a daemon job.
.TP
.B cat \fR[\fB\-\-\fR] \fIfile\fR...
Fetch remote file contents for display, using the pager for long output
when enabled. Multiple operands are read in order.
.TP
.B cd \fR[\fIdirectory\fR]
Change the remote working directory. Without an argument, return to the
connection's initial directory.
.TP
.B close
Alias for
.BR disconnect .
.TP
.B connect \fIurl\fR|\fIname
Connect to a URL or a bookmark. The URL scheme selects the backend;
bookmarks may specify a backend explicitly. See
.B PROTOCOLS
and
.BR BOOKMARKS .
.TP
.B detach
Detach the terminal. Running daemon jobs continue; the daemon remains
only while jobs are running. Reattach using
.BR "xfer attach" .
.TP
.B disconnect
Release the active backend connection.
.TP
.B du \fR[\fB\-sh\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIpath\fR...]
Recursively report remote apparent byte usage, not allocated disk blocks.
The default path is the current remote directory.
.B \-s
reports only totals for the requested roots;
.B \-h
uses human-readable sizes.
.TP
.B exit
Alias for
.BR detach .
.TP
.BI fg " job-id"
Bring a daemon job into the client's foreground and wait for it.
.TP
.B get \fR[\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIfile\fR...
Download remote files into the daemon's local working directory using
their basenames. Patterns select files in the current remote directory.
Existing local files are protected unless
.B xfer:clobber
is true.
.B \-c
continues an existing local file from its current length. A local file
larger than its remote counterpart cannot be resumed. Directories must
be downloaded with
.BR mirror .
.TP
.B help
List available commands and aliases.
.TP
.B jobs
List daemon jobs with their IDs, states, and titles.
.TP
.B lcd \fR[\fIdirectory\fR]
Change the local working directory used by transfers and Bash escapes.
Without an argument, use the user's home directory. Local paths may use
.B ~
or
.BR ~/ .
The initial local directory is the directory from which the daemon started.
.TP
.B ls \fR[\fB\-FlarSh\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
List remote entries. Without a path, list the current directory. A path
may name a directory, a file, or a supported filename pattern.
Default options come from
.BR ui:ls-default-options .
.RS
.TP
.B \-F
Append file-type indicators.
.TP
.B \-l
Use the long listing format.
.TP
.B \-a
Include hidden entries.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.B \-S
Sort by size, largest first.
.TP
.B \-h
Display human-readable sizes.
.RE
.IP
Normally directories sort before files, with names sorted within each
group. Command options are applied after the defaults. Use
.BR \-\-no-classify ,
.BR \-\-no-long ,
.BR \-\-no-all ,
.BR \-\-no-reverse ,
.BR \-\-no-size ,
or
.B \-\-no-human-readable
to disable the corresponding option.
.TP
.B mirror \fR[\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIdirectory\fR...
Recursively download named remote directories below the local working
directory, preserving each directory's basename. Existing regular files
are skipped by default, or overwritten when
.B xfer:clobber
is true.
.B \-c
resumes existing files. This is a recursive copy, not a synchronization
engine: it does not delete extra destination files or compare timestamps
like lftp's mirror command. Remote root, dot, and dot-dot are not accepted
as root directory names.
.TP
.B mirror-upload \fR[\fB\-\-\fR] \fIdirectory\fR...
Recursively upload local directories below the current remote directory,
preserving their basenames. Replacement of existing remote files is
controlled by
.BR xfer:clobber .
This command has no resume option and does not delete extra remote files.
.TP
.B mirror-up
Alias for
.BR mirror-upload .
.TP
.B mkdir \fR[\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIdirectory\fR...
Create remote directories.
.BR \-p " (" \-\-parents )
creates missing parents and accepts existing directories. S3 directories
are trailing-slash marker objects; this command does not create buckets.
.TP
.B mv \fR[\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIsource destination
Rename a remote file to an exact destination path, not a destination
directory. Directory moves are not supported.
.BR \-f " (" \-\-force )
permits replacement of an existing file for this operation; otherwise
.B xfer:clobber
controls replacement. S3 implements this as copy followed by delete,
which is not atomic.
.TP
.B open \fIurl\fR|\fIname
Alias for
.BR connect .
.TP
.B put \fR[\fB\-\-\fR] \fIfile\fR...
Upload local files into the current remote directory using their basenames.
Patterns select regular files in the current local directory. Existing
remote files are protected unless
.B xfer:clobber
is true. Use
.B mirror-upload
for directories.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the remote working directory.
.TP
.B rm \fR[\fB\-\-\fR] \fIfile\fR...
Delete remote files. This command is non-recursive and does not accept
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI set " name value"
Change a daemon setting. Quote values containing spaces; use an empty
quoted string for an empty text value. Changes are session-local and are
not written to a configuration file. Connection settings generally take
effect on a new connection.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fIname\fR]
Show all settings or a single setting, with descriptions. Password values
are redacted.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fIjob-id\fR]
Without an argument, show daemon, connection, local and remote directory,
and configuration-file information. With a job ID, show job details.
.SH PROTOCOLS
.TP
.B s3://\fIbucket
Use S3 with the default AWS credential chain unless bookmark credentials
are supplied. Use
.B s3:endpoint
or an HTTP(S) S3 bookmark for an S3-compatible service. Buckets must
already exist. Only Signature Version 4 is supported.
.TP
.B sftp://\fR[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR][\fB/\fIpath\fR]
Use SFTP through OpenSSH. SSH configuration, keys, and ssh-agent are
normally used for authentication. Host-key checking is strict by default.
Password bookmarks support a single password exchange, not
keyboard-interactive MFA or challenge-response authentication.
.TP
.B smb://\fR[\fIdomain\fB;\fR][\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR][\fB/\fIshare\fR[\fB/\fIpath\fR]]
Use SMB. A server URL without a share can be used to browse shares.
An empty username means anonymous access, not the operating-system user.
.TP
.B http://\fIhost/path/\fR, \fBhttps://\fIhost/path/
Use WebDAV. The schemes
.BR dav ,
.BR davs ,
.BR webdav ,
and
.B webdavs
are also supported; the variants ending in "s" use TLS.
The server must support WebDAV PROPFIND; an HTML directory index is
insufficient. Authentication is anonymous or Basic, not Digest, NTLM,
OAuth/Bearer, or client certificates. Plaintext HTTP credentials are
refused unless explicitly enabled.
.PP
HTTP(S) bookmarks select S3 instead of WebDAV when they specify
.BR "type = 's3'" ,
a
.B bucket
field, or an
.B accessKey
field. An explicit bookmark type takes precedence over inference.
.SH SETTINGS
Settings are named with colon-separated namespaces. Boolean values are
.B true
or
.BR false ;
timeouts are in seconds. The defaults are shown below. Use
.B show
for current values.
.TP
.B bookmarks:file \fR(path)
Bookmark file. Default: bookmarks.toml in the application configuration
directory.
.TP
.B cache:max-bytes \fR(integer)
Per-connection directory cache budget in bytes. Default: 4194304.
Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B cache:ttl \fR(integer)
Directory cache lifetime in seconds; zero disables caching. Default: 30.
Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B s3:endpoint \fR(string)
Optional S3-compatible HTTP(S) endpoint. Default: empty.
.TP
.B s3:region \fR(string)
Default S3 signing region. Default: us-east-1.
.TP
.B sftp:connect-timeout \fR(integer)
Positive SSH connection and authentication timeout. Default: 30.
.TP
.B sftp:disable-strict-checking \fR(boolean)
Disable strict SSH host-key checking for new connections. Default: false.
This permits unverified unknown host keys and is insecure; OpenSSH may
still restrict authentication when a known host key changes.
.TP
.B sftp:max-requests \fR(integer)
Concurrent SFTP read/write requests, from 1 to 256. Default: 64.
Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B smb:connect-timeout \fR(integer)
SMB connection and login timeout. Default: 10.
.TP
.B smb:operation-timeout \fR(integer)
SMB operation timeout. Default: 30.
.TP
.B smb:max-credits \fR(integer)
Maximum in-flight 64 KiB transfer chunks, from 1 to 128. Default: 32.
Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B smb:user \fR(string)
Default SMB username; empty selects anonymous access. Default: empty.
.TP
.B smb:password \fR(secret)
Default SMB password, displayed redacted. Default: unset. Prefer
per-bookmark credentials or interactive entry.
.TP
.B smb:domain \fR(string)
Default NTLM domain. Default: empty.
.TP
.B smb:dfs \fR(boolean)
Allow DFS referrals to other servers, forwarding credentials to them.
Default: false. Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B smb:require-encryption \fR(boolean)
Refuse shares without active SMB3 encryption. Default: false.
Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B ssl:skip-validation \fR(boolean)
Disable TLS certificate validation. Default: false. Enabling this is
insecure and permits man-in-the-middle attacks.
.TP
.B ui:auto-pager \fR(boolean)
Use the built-in pager for long output. Default: true.
.TP
.B ui:history-file \fR(path)
Persistent command history file. Default: history in the application state
directory. The values
.B session
and
.B /dev/null
disable persistence. The client reads this setting when attaching.
.TP
.B ui:ls-default-options \fR(string)
Base options for
.BR ls .
Default: \-lF. Only options, not a path, are allowed.
.TP
.B ui:prompt \fR(string)
Prompt template. Default: "xfer$location> ". Substitutions are
.B $cwd
(protocol and remote path),
.B $cwdn
(remote basename),
.BR $user ,
.BR $host ,
.BR $proto ,
.B $lcwd
(local path),
.B $lcwdn
(local basename), and
.B $location
(bracketed protocol and remote path, empty when disconnected).
Braced forms such as
.B ${host}
are accepted;
.B $$
produces a literal dollar sign. No shell code is evaluated.
.TP
.B webdav:user \fR(string)
Default WebDAV Basic-auth username; empty means anonymous. Default: empty.
.TP
.B webdav:password \fR(secret)
Default WebDAV password, displayed redacted. Default: unset.
.TP
.B webdav:allow-insecure-auth \fR(boolean)
Allow Basic credentials over plaintext HTTP. Default: false. This exposes
credentials to the network. Reconnect to apply.
.TP
.B webdav:connect-timeout \fR(integer)
TCP connection timeout, from 1 to 300 seconds. Default: 10.
.TP
.B webdav:operation-timeout \fR(integer)
Request/read timeout, from 1 to 300 seconds. Default: 30.
.TP
.B xfer:clobber \fR(boolean)
Allow downloads to overwrite local files and uploads or renames to replace
remote files. Default: false. Recursive downloads have their own
skip/resume behavior; see
.BR mirror .
.SH CONFIGURATION
At daemon startup, xfer reads
.I xfer.conf
from its application configuration directory, then reads the file named by
.B XFER_CONFIG
if set. Later values override earlier values. Missing optional files are
ignored. Configuration lines have the form
.IR "name: value" ;
blank lines and lines whose first non-space character is # are ignored.
Values are literal text, not shell-quoted command arguments.
.PP
.nf
.RS
# xfer.conf
ui:auto-pager: true
ui:ls-default-options: -lhF
s3:region: us-east-1
sftp:max-requests: 64
ssl:skip-validation: false
.RE
.fi
.PP
On the first attachment to a daemon, the client also reads
.I ~/.xferrc
and executes its commands in order, one command per line. Blank lines and
comment lines are ignored. This file uses command syntax, not the
configuration-file setting syntax. It is not rerun on later attachments.
For example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# ~/.xferrc
set ui:prompt 'xfer$location> '
lcd ~/Downloads
.RE
.fi
.SH BOOKMARKS
Bookmarks are TOML tables in the file selected by
.BR bookmarks:file .
Each table name is an alias accepted by
.BR open .
The
.B url
field is required;
.B type
may be
.BR s3 ,
.BR sftp ,
.BR smb ,
or
.BR webdav .
When omitted, the type is inferred from the URL and S3-specific fields.
Unknown fields are rejected.
.PP
.nf
.RS
[archive]
type = 's3'
url = 's3://example-archive'
region = 'us-east-1'

[minio]
type = 's3'
url = 'https://minio.example.net'
bucket = 'backups'
accessKey = 'replace-me'
secretKey = 'replace-me'
api = 's3v4'

[home]
url = 'sftp://alice@sftp.example.net/home/alice'

[nas]
url = 'smb://nas.example.net/Documents'
user = 'alice'
domain = 'WORKGROUP'
password_interactive = true

[web-files]
url = 'https://dav.example.net/files/'
user = 'alice'
password_interactive = true
.RE
.fi
.PP
S3 fields are
.BR bucket ,
.BR region ,
.BR accessKey ,
.BR secretKey ,
.BR sessionToken ,
.BR api ,
.BR override_host ,
and
.BR override_port .
Access and secret keys must be supplied together. Without explicit
credentials, the AWS credential chain is used.
.PP
SFTP, SMB, and WebDAV bookmarks may store a
.B password
or set
.B password_interactive = true
to request a password in the terminal. SMB and WebDAV also support
.BR user ;
SMB supports
.BR domain .
Explicit empty user fields select anonymous access. For SFTP, specify the
username in the URL.
.PP
Protect files containing credentials, for example with mode 0600.
Prefer interactive passwords to plaintext storage. Credential-bearing
connection commands and password-setting commands are excluded from
history, but shell escapes and other command text may still contain
secrets: do not rely on history filtering as a general secret scrubber.
.SH TERMINAL INTERFACE
At the prompt, Tab completes commands, bookmarks, settings, and applicable
local or remote paths. Up and Down navigate command history. Ctrl-A and
Ctrl-E move to the beginning and end of the line. Ctrl-L redraws the screen.
Ctrl-C clears the current input; Ctrl-D on an empty line detaches.
.PP
Ctrl-S opens the context-sensitive file selector. Use arrows or j/k to
move, Page Up/Down or Ctrl-B/Ctrl-F to scroll, Home/End for boundaries,
Space to mark entries where multiple selection is available, and Enter
to accept. Slash starts filtering. Escape or Ctrl-C cancels.
.PP
In the built-in pager, use arrows or j/k for lines, Space, Page Down, or
Ctrl-F for the next page, Page Up or Ctrl-B for the previous page, and
Home/End for the beginning/end. Press q or Escape to quit. The vertical
bar key opens a private copy of the output in
.BR VISUAL ,
then
.BR EDITOR ,
or
.BR vi ;
editing the copy does not change the pager's data.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XFER_CONFIG
Additional settings file, read after the default xfer.conf at daemon startup.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If absolute, places configuration in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xfer.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
If absolute, places disposable data in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/xfer.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
If absolute, places persistent local state in $XDG_STATE_HOME/xfer.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
If absolute, places daemon discovery files and sockets in
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xfer/instances. Otherwise the native runtime directory
is used if available, falling back to the state directory's instances
subdirectory. Empty or relative XDG overrides are ignored.
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Editor commands for bookmark editing and pager output copies.
.TP
.B AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_SESSION_TOKEN, AWS_PROFILE
Used by the standard AWS credential provider chain when bookmark
credentials are not supplied.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Used by OpenSSH for ssh-agent authentication.
.SH FILES
The following are Linux defaults. Absolute XDG overrides take precedence.
On macOS, configuration and persistent state normally use
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/xfer" ,
and cache data uses
.IR ~/Library/Caches/xfer .
.TP
.I ~/.config/xfer/xfer.conf
Daemon settings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/xfer/bookmarks.toml
Connection bookmarks; overridable with
.BR bookmarks:file .
.TP
.I ~/.xferrc
Commands run on the first attachment to a daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/xfer/history
Persistent command history; overridable with
.BR ui:history-file .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/xfer/jobs/
Daemon job output spools.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xfer/instances/
Private daemon sockets and discovery files when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is set.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config, ~/.ssh/known_hosts
OpenSSH client configuration and known host keys used by SFTP.
.SH EXAMPLES
Connect to a bookmark, download a file, and upload a local file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ xfer
xfer> open home
xfer[sftp:/home/alice/]> ls
xfer[sftp:/home/alice/]> get report.pdf
xfer[sftp:/home/alice/]> put local-notes.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Resume a download and recursively copy a directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
lcd ~/Downloads
get -c large-image.iso
mirror -c photos
.RE
.fi
.PP
Upload a local directory, explicitly allowing replacement:
.PP
.nf
.RS
set xfer:clobber true
mirror-upload documents
set xfer:clobber false
.RE
.fi
.PP
After Ctrl-Z backgrounds a transfer, inspect it and detach:
.PP
.nf
.RS
jobs
status 1
detach
.RE
.fi
.PP
From another terminal, while the detached daemon still has running jobs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ xfer attach
.RE
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
S3 has no real directories. File moves use server-side copy followed by
delete and can leave both objects after failure or cancellation. Objects
up to 5 TiB are supported; uploads and copies larger than 5 GiB use
multipart operations. Multipart copy also requires permission to read
object tags and abort multipart uploads. Directory/prefix moves are not
implemented.
.PP
Failed or cancelled multipart uploads and copies are aborted on a
best-effort basis. Configure S3 lifecycle cleanup for incomplete multipart
operations to avoid ongoing storage charges. If completion fails, check
the destination before retrying because completion may have succeeded.
Other backends may also leave partial files after failed uploads.
.PP
Disabling TLS validation or strict SSH host-key checking compromises peer
authentication. Sending Basic credentials over HTTP exposes them in
plaintext. Keep the secure defaults unless the risks are understood.
